<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Assassination of Abraham Lincoln</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1865">1865</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The assassination of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abraham-lincoln">Abraham Lincoln</a> took place on 14 April 1865, five days after the surrender of the main Confederate army, when the actor and Confederate sympathiser John Wilkes Booth shot the president at Ford's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">Theatre</a> in Washington; Lincoln died the next morning. It was part of a conspiracy that also gravely wounded Secretary of State William Seward. Lincoln's successor, Andrew Johnson, pursued a lenient policy towards the defeated South that brought him into bitter conflict with Congress over Reconstruction.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>John Wilkes Booth, born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maryland">Maryland</a> into a family of prominent stage actors, was a national celebrity and an outspoken Confederate sympathiser. With Union victory imminent, he and conspirators including Lewis Powell, David Herold and George Atzerodt first plotted to kidnap Lincoln to aid the Confederacy. When that failed, they decided to kill Lincoln, Secretary of State William H. Seward and Vice President Andrew Johnson, hoping that removing the three most important federal officials would revive the Confederate cause.</p><p>Accounts of Booth's motives differ. In a letter to his mother he wrote of avenging the South. On 11 April he attended Lincoln's last speech, in which Lincoln promoted voting rights for emancipated slaves, and in anger urged Powell to shoot Lincoln on the spot. Powell refused for fear of the crowd.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>On 14 April Booth learned at Ford's Theatre that Lincoln was to attend that evening's performance of Our American Cousin. Booth knew the theatre's layout and its staff. Major Henry Rathbone and his fiancée Clara Harris joined the Lincolns, and the president's usual protection was absent.</p><p>At about 10:25 pm Booth entered the presidential box and shot Lincoln in the head from behind. Rathbone struggled with him and was stabbed in the arm, and Booth jumped to the stage, a drop of about twelve feet, and fled. Witness accounts conflict over what he shouted; most remembered Sic semper tyrannis. The surgeon Charles Leale found a wound behind the left ear and pronounced it mortal. Lincoln was carried to the Petersen House across the street and died the next morning. Elsewhere Powell attacked Seward at his home and wounded him. Atzerodt got drunk and never approached Johnson.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Booth crossed into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maryland">Maryland</a> and, with Herold, was tracked by the largest manhunt in US history at the time, directed by Secretary of War Edwin Stanton. On 26 April they were found at a tobacco farm in King George County, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a>, where Sergeant Boston Corbett shot Booth. A military tribunal tried the other accused. Powell, Herold, Atzerodt and Mary Surratt were hanged in July 1865. John Surratt, who fled abroad, was captured in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in November 1866 and released after a mistrial in 1867.</p><p>Johnson was sworn in as the 17th president on 15 April. Lincoln was the first US president to be assassinated. A train carried his remains over 1,700 miles through seven states to burial in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illinois">Illinois</a>, with millions lining the route. Mourning spread to the South and around the world, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walt-whitman">Walt Whitman</a> wrote poems for him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Assassination of Abraham Lincoln” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
